WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:05.467
Simone Stolzoff: There was a study

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:07.934
that was done in the University
College London,

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:11.934
where researchers split
participants into two groups,

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:15.401
and in one group they were given
an100-percent chance

00:00:15.434 --> 00:00:18.801
of receiving a really
painful electric shock.

00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:20.634
And then the other group,

00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:24.967
they were given a 50-percent chance
of receiving a painful electric shock.

00:00:25.401 --> 00:00:28.967
And fascinatingly, they found
that people in the second group,

00:00:29.001 --> 00:00:31.001
people who had a 50-percent chance,

00:00:31.001 --> 00:00:33.867
were far more anxious and stressed

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.501
than people in the 100-percent
chance group.

00:00:36.501 --> 00:00:40.834
So somehow, we would rather
a certain bad thing happen to us

00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:44.867
than have to deal with the ambiguity
of not being sure.

00:00:44.901 --> 00:00:46.601
I'm curious, from your perspective,

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:48.801
what are the implications
of a study like this?

00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:50.567
What does it say about our societal

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:53.801
or our individual ability
to navigate what we don't know?

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.467
Maya Shankar: Yeah, I mean, I think

00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:59.867
it makes so much sense
that we as humans, you know,

00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:01.401
evolved to be this way.

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:05.201
It's very helpful for us
to be prediction machines

00:01:05.234 --> 00:01:07.734
that are constantly trying
to anticipate future risks

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.467
and trying to get ahead of them
and solve for the future.

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:13.034
The downside to that
is that there are lots of changes

00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:15.167
that are truly outside of our control,

00:01:15.201 --> 00:01:19.801
and no amount of planning
will rescue us from those situations.

00:01:19.834 --> 00:01:22.601
And in those moments,
our brains can kind of short-circuit.

00:01:22.634 --> 00:01:27.601
I definitely feel like
that study describes me to a tee.

00:01:27.601 --> 00:01:30.101
I would rather know how the story ends.

00:01:30.134 --> 00:01:32.501
I would rather have clarity
on the outcome.

00:01:33.034 --> 00:01:36.434
I was just actually reading a book
for an upcoming podcast interview

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:37.934
on "A Slight Change of Plans."

00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:39.634
And this woman cited a study.

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:41.634
She's a physician.

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:46.934
And she was talking about how people

00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:49.734
are way more stressed
during that intervening period

00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:52.567
when they're not sure
what the medical result is

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:54.367
when they're waiting on a test result

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:57.401
than they are when they actually
find out what the disease is.

00:01:57.401 --> 00:01:59.201
And that was really interesting to me

00:01:59.234 --> 00:02:01.067
because when I reflect back on my life

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:04.134
at the moments when I felt
the most deeply anxious,

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:07.334
the most unsure, my life and my --

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:10.834
and when my well-being
has been most compromised,

00:02:10.867 --> 00:02:14.434
it is in those periods
where there is some uncertainty.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:16.967
And I wonder if it's because we --

00:02:18.001 --> 00:02:20.634
because we lack that agency
in those moments,

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:23.134
we're just grasping to try to figure out

00:02:23.134 --> 00:02:25.301
how we might be able
to change the situation.

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:27.901
And in moments of uncertainty,

00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:31.601
we have the illusion that our involvement
could actually move the needle.

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:34.034
But after we've received
the actual information,

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:35.834
it feels more set in stone.

00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:38.401
SS: I think this is very relatable

00:02:38.401 --> 00:02:42.867
for anyone who is dealing
with maybe professional uncertainty

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:46.034
or uncertainty about what
decisions they want to make

00:02:46.034 --> 00:02:47.267
about their future lives.

00:02:47.301 --> 00:02:50.501
I think we can think about this
from an evolutionary perspective.

00:02:50.534 --> 00:02:54.234
So if you imagine an ancestor
of ours in the jungle,

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:56.201
if they hear a rustle in the bushes

00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:58.834
and they're not sure
the source of that noise,

00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:01.567
that uncertainty
could potentially be fatal.

00:03:01.901 --> 00:03:06.767
And so our brains are wired
to feel safe and secure

00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:08.434
when we are certain,

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:12.601
and to feel incredibly uncomfortable
when we are uncertain.

00:03:12.967 --> 00:03:15.101
And in general,
this is a survival instinct.

00:03:15.134 --> 00:03:16.434
It can steer us well.

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:18.634
It can incentivize us to, say,

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:21.101
put on a seat belt
before we enter a car ride.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:24.034
There's been similar studies
for breast cancer patients

00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:27.634
who have found that the period
between getting a biopsy

00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:29.334
and getting the diagnosis

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:31.901
is often the hardest part
of the entire process,

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:34.201
harder than chemo or surgery.

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:36.701
Because once we know
what we're dealing with,

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:38.934
then we know where to direct our energy.

00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:42.967
But before we have that certainty,
we can't necessarily plan.

00:03:43.001 --> 00:03:47.034
We can't necessarily know where
we should be spending our resources.

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:51.434
But I think there's also a cost to this
discomfort we have with uncertainty,

00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:56.301
which is that we might close our minds off
to new possibilities.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:59.601
I think we all probably have a friend
who's maybe in a job

00:03:59.634 --> 00:04:03.901
or in a relationship that they know
isn't quite working for them,

00:04:03.934 --> 00:04:08.534
but they're so uncomfortable
with the uncertainty of leaving

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:10.301
that they sort of grit their teeth

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:12.067
and just maintain the status quo.

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:16.201
And in my mind, that is
sort of the main downside

00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:18.367
of our intolerance of uncertainty.

00:04:18.401 --> 00:04:21.067
It's that when we are certain
it has a narrowing effect,

00:04:21.101 --> 00:04:22.534
it closes our minds

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:25.667
when we think we know exactly
who's going to win the election

00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:29.367
or exactly how the market
is going to react in the future.

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:32.367
But when we're able to maintain
a state of not knowing,

00:04:32.401 --> 00:04:34.834
we're able to see the possibilities

00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:38.867
that may be sitting
on the other side of our discomfort.

00:04:38.901 --> 00:04:40.501
There's one other element to this

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:43.067
that I think you are particularly
primed to speak on,

00:04:43.101 --> 00:04:46.734
which is the relationship
between identity and uncertainty

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:48.634
or identity and change.

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:53.367
What do you think is so hard about change
when it comes to our self-conception?

00:04:54.201 --> 00:04:55.634
MS: Yeah, it's so interesting.

00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:58.667
There was a moment in hosting
"A Slight Change of Plans"

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:00.667
where, I remember I was going on a walk,

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:02.934
and I realized for the first time,

00:05:02.967 --> 00:05:06.467
“Oh my God, this is not even
really a show about change --

00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:07.967
It’s a show about identity.”

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:11.001
And that insight was born from the fact

00:05:11.034 --> 00:05:13.501
that so many of the stories I was hearing

00:05:13.501 --> 00:05:17.934
revealed that one of the biggest reasons
why change is so scary

00:05:17.967 --> 00:05:22.067
is because it can threaten
our self-identity

00:05:22.101 --> 00:05:25.467
in these really potent ways
that are very uncomfortable.

00:05:25.501 --> 00:05:28.167
Now let's think about
self-identity for a moment.

00:05:28.801 --> 00:05:31.867
It's very helpful for us to carry them,
to have these identities,

00:05:31.901 --> 00:05:34.534
these labels, these roles
we give ourselves, right?

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:37.567
If I identify, for example,
as an athlete --

00:05:37.567 --> 00:05:40.867
it's funny I've chosen that
because I so don't identify as an athlete.

00:05:40.901 --> 00:05:43.734
That's actually the last thing
I'm qualified to identify as.

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:47.901
But while we're in this magical world
together on a Thursday,

00:05:47.934 --> 00:05:50.001
let's just pretend that I'm an athlete.

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:55.734
It gives me an immediate feeling
of camaraderie with my fellow athletes.

00:05:55.734 --> 00:05:58.467
So I feel a sense of community
and group membership.

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:01.467
I feel meaning and purpose
in everyday life, right?

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:03.901
Each day is imbued with a mission

00:06:03.934 --> 00:06:08.534
which can help stave off existential
dread and feelings of nihilism.

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:12.801
So there's a lot of advantages
to carrying these identities.

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:17.801
The problem is that when
an unexpected change happens to us,

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:22.501
it can threaten that identity
and we can feel very unmoored.

00:06:22.534 --> 00:06:25.201
So I experienced this actually
when I was a little kid.

00:06:25.234 --> 00:06:28.067
I was an aspiring concert violinist.

00:06:28.701 --> 00:06:30.401
I started playing when I was six

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:34.401
and I studied at the “Juilliard School
of Music” under Itzhak Perlman.

00:06:34.434 --> 00:06:38.367
And I had really big dreams
of one day becoming a professional.

00:06:38.867 --> 00:06:43.001
And then my "slight change of plans"
was that I had a career-ending injury.

00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:47.301
And I remember that there was something
so curious about my grief

00:06:47.301 --> 00:06:49.367
when it came to the loss of the violin,

00:06:49.401 --> 00:06:54.167
which is that I found myself grieving
not just the loss of the instrument,

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:57.601
but also the loss of myself
in this much deeper way.

00:06:57.634 --> 00:07:00.767
I think many of us don't realize sometimes

00:07:00.801 --> 00:07:05.734
how much something has come to define us
until we lose that very thing.

00:07:05.767 --> 00:07:08.667
And I realized that so much of who I was

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:11.701
had become entangled
with this identity of violinist.

00:07:12.767 --> 00:07:13.934
My sense of belonging,

00:07:13.967 --> 00:07:16.334
you know, I was bullied
a lot in my school,

00:07:16.367 --> 00:07:20.001
I was one of the few brown kids
in a predominantly Caucasian school.

00:07:20.001 --> 00:07:23.034
So I found refuge in the more
international community

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:24.567
of my music school.

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.367
A lot of my self-confidence
was tied up in it.

00:07:27.401 --> 00:07:30.601
And so when I lost the violin,
all of that kind of went away

00:07:30.634 --> 00:07:36.901
and I felt myself feeling really
untethered from anything meaningful.

00:07:37.301 --> 00:07:42.201
And it's taken me decades to learn
a valuable lesson from that,

00:07:42.234 --> 00:07:44.634
that I'm so excited to share
with all of you today.

00:07:44.667 --> 00:07:46.867
Because had I known it at the time,

00:07:46.901 --> 00:07:50.567
I think I would have had an easier
transition point for myself.

00:07:50.934 --> 00:07:54.234
And that is it can be quite
precarious for us

00:07:54.267 --> 00:07:59.467
to anchor our self-identity
too tightly to what we do.

00:08:00.301 --> 00:08:03.067
Instead, we can learn
to anchor our identities

00:08:03.101 --> 00:08:05.734
to why we do the things we do.

00:08:06.234 --> 00:08:09.201
So when I ask myself this question,
when it came to the violin,

00:08:09.234 --> 00:08:13.467
I realized, oh, human connection
was at the core of my love for music.

00:08:13.501 --> 00:08:16.801
I love connecting with my peers,
I love connecting with the audience.

00:08:16.801 --> 00:08:19.234
And importantly,
just because I lost the violin

00:08:19.267 --> 00:08:22.634
did not mean that I lost what led me
to love it in the first place.

00:08:22.667 --> 00:08:25.301
That part of Maya was still
very much intact,

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:28.667
even as a 15-year-old who could
no longer play her instrument.

00:08:29.034 --> 00:08:32.201
And when you anchor
your identity in your why,

00:08:32.234 --> 00:08:34.234
then you have a path forward,

00:08:34.267 --> 00:08:36.834
because the thought experiment becomes,

00:08:36.834 --> 00:08:38.601
well, through what other means,

00:08:38.634 --> 00:08:41.867
through what other outlets
can I express this part of who I am?

00:08:41.901 --> 00:08:46.567
And it turns out I kind of subconsciously
gravitated towards those spaces.

00:08:47.201 --> 00:08:51.067
I became a cognitive scientist who studies
the science of human connection.

00:08:51.634 --> 00:08:54.367
In hosting "A Slight Change of Plans."

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:57.834
It's all about forging deep,
emotional connections with the people

00:08:57.867 --> 00:08:59.067
that I'm interviewing.

00:08:59.067 --> 00:09:01.734
This is the same for writing
"The Other Side of Change,"

00:09:01.734 --> 00:09:04.634
where I spent years interviewing
people on repeated occasions

00:09:04.667 --> 00:09:08.767
and really going deep and deep
and deep when it came to their stories.

00:09:09.167 --> 00:09:12.334
And so I've been able

00:09:12.367 --> 00:09:17.034
to actually still express this huge
part of what makes Maya Maya.

00:09:17.767 --> 00:09:21.467
And to hold on to that
as kind of a north star,

00:09:21.501 --> 00:09:24.501
a compass of sorts that can guide me
towards my next steps.

00:09:24.534 --> 00:09:27.567
And I feel like that has helped me,

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:33.167
that why part of my self-identity
has helped me weather more recent changes

00:09:33.201 --> 00:09:36.634
with a bit more equanimity,
a bit more ease.

00:09:36.667 --> 00:09:41.034
And that's because I know that the world
can't take that passion away.

00:09:41.767 --> 00:09:43.234
It's still very much there.

00:09:43.234 --> 00:09:46.434
And so I would urge people
who are tuning in today

00:09:46.467 --> 00:09:49.401
to ask themselves what their why is.

00:09:50.967 --> 00:09:55.134
Maybe it's service,
maybe it's getting better at a skill.

00:09:55.167 --> 00:09:56.901
Maybe it's having a creative outlet.

00:09:56.934 --> 00:10:00.101
Whatever your why is,
that can be a soft landing

00:10:00.134 --> 00:10:02.234
when life makes other plans for you

00:10:02.267 --> 00:10:05.601
and can help you steer yourself
towards what comes next.

00:10:05.634 --> 00:10:07.034
SS: I love that so much.

00:10:07.067 --> 00:10:10.901
I think there's an element
to it that is right in line

00:10:10.934 --> 00:10:14.267
with the topic of my first book,
which is called "The Good Enough Job."

00:10:14.301 --> 00:10:17.801
And the argument there was about the value
of diversifying our identities,

00:10:17.834 --> 00:10:21.701
of not seeing ourselves as just one thing.

00:10:21.701 --> 00:10:25.401
And one thing that anchoring
your identity to your why

00:10:25.434 --> 00:10:27.667
as opposed to your what does

00:10:27.667 --> 00:10:30.767
is it is much more robust

00:10:30.767 --> 00:10:33.967
in the face of the changing
winds around you.

00:10:34.434 --> 00:10:37.034
So in the pandemic, for example,

00:10:37.067 --> 00:10:41.567
I spoke to so many workers
who completely identified with their job,

00:10:41.601 --> 00:10:45.634
maybe being a Googler or working
for this particular organization.

00:10:45.967 --> 00:10:48.167
And then the market changed,

00:10:48.201 --> 00:10:51.901
or there was a series
of layoffs or furloughs,

00:10:51.934 --> 00:10:53.934
and they were left asking themselves,

00:10:53.967 --> 00:10:56.834
"If I don't have my job, who am I?"

00:10:57.467 --> 00:11:00.434
There's two people in particular
that I'm thinking about

00:11:00.467 --> 00:11:01.767
in this conversation.

00:11:01.767 --> 00:11:05.367
One is this woman named Liz,
who I interviewed for my first book,

00:11:05.401 --> 00:11:09.101
and she was sort of your typical
type-A overachiever.

00:11:09.134 --> 00:11:11.301
She was a D1 college athlete,

00:11:11.334 --> 00:11:13.701
actually, she was a two-sport
D1 college athlete.

00:11:13.701 --> 00:11:16.634
She was both on the water
polo team and a swimmer.

00:11:16.667 --> 00:11:17.834
Exactly.

00:11:17.834 --> 00:11:19.267
And then she graduated.

00:11:19.301 --> 00:11:20.567
She did Teach for America,

00:11:20.567 --> 00:11:21.934
she found a lot of identity

00:11:21.934 --> 00:11:24.301
through being a public school
teacher in Colorado.

00:11:24.334 --> 00:11:27.434
Then she went to law school,
found a lot of identity through law

00:11:27.467 --> 00:11:28.701
and being a lawyer.

00:11:28.701 --> 00:11:30.367
But in that period of time,

00:11:30.401 --> 00:11:32.934
she contracted a chronic illness.

00:11:33.267 --> 00:11:35.334
And so she went from someone
who could, say,

00:11:35.334 --> 00:11:37.567
swim four to six hours a day

00:11:37.601 --> 00:11:41.734
to her mom spoon-feeding her
chicken soup in bed.

00:11:41.767 --> 00:11:47.334
And she couldn't any longer
derive her self-worth or her identity

00:11:47.367 --> 00:11:49.667
from her external accomplishments.

00:11:49.701 --> 00:11:52.834
She always prided herself
on being a straight-A student

00:11:52.867 --> 00:11:55.134
or getting great performance reviews.

00:11:55.167 --> 00:11:58.767
But as she started talking
to more people with chronic illnesses,

00:11:58.767 --> 00:12:00.534
she learned this thing about herself,

00:12:00.567 --> 00:12:04.734
which is you can define yourself
based on your evergreen traits

00:12:04.767 --> 00:12:07.134
as opposed to your accomplishments.

00:12:07.167 --> 00:12:11.667
And so she started conceiving
of herself as a generous friend

00:12:11.701 --> 00:12:15.134
or someone who really believes
in particular causes

00:12:15.167 --> 00:12:17.234
and right in line with what
you were saying,

00:12:17.234 --> 00:12:18.734
she connected to her why,

00:12:18.767 --> 00:12:22.901
a why that no employer or market

00:12:22.934 --> 00:12:25.567
or boss could ever take away from her.

00:12:25.601 --> 00:12:29.467
And it became a much more
durative identity

00:12:29.501 --> 00:12:32.901
that could shift along
with the changes around her.

00:12:32.934 --> 00:12:34.467
And that's really stuck with me.

00:12:34.501 --> 00:12:36.834
It's one of those ideas
that even though it came

00:12:36.834 --> 00:12:39.234
from the chronically-ill community,

00:12:39.234 --> 00:12:41.801
it's something we can all
apply to our own lives

00:12:41.834 --> 00:12:46.434
of thinking about what are our traits,
our evergreen characteristics.

00:12:46.467 --> 00:12:51.567
And rather than seeing ourselves
as just an accountant or a lawyer

00:12:51.601 --> 00:12:55.334
or someone who is very impressive
in this way or that way,

00:12:55.367 --> 00:12:57.901
what are some of those values or virtues

00:12:57.901 --> 00:13:01.434
that will remain constant
amidst all of the change?

00:13:02.267 --> 00:13:04.534
MS: I love that story so much.

00:13:05.734 --> 00:13:09.234
I think the argument
from both of our books

00:13:09.234 --> 00:13:12.467
is that there is so much possibility

00:13:12.501 --> 00:13:15.601
that sits within uncertainty,
that sits within change.

00:13:16.034 --> 00:13:21.634
And one of the reasons that I wanted
to write "The Other Side of Change"

00:13:21.667 --> 00:13:26.134
is that, at moments
of inflection in my life,

00:13:26.167 --> 00:13:27.967
whether it was losing the violin

00:13:27.967 --> 00:13:31.601
or struggling to start
a family with my husband

00:13:31.634 --> 00:13:34.167
and facing pregnancy losses
with our surrogate

00:13:34.167 --> 00:13:37.201
and other disappointments

00:13:37.234 --> 00:13:39.767
and obstacles,

00:13:39.801 --> 00:13:42.401
I would hear so often this popular refrain

00:13:42.434 --> 00:13:46.334
that while you can't change
what happens to you,

00:13:46.334 --> 00:13:48.567
you can change your response
to what happens.

00:13:48.601 --> 00:13:50.901
You can change your reaction
to what happens.

00:13:50.934 --> 00:13:54.667
And it's meant to be empowering, Simone,

00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:57.834
but I felt frustrated
when I would hear that.

00:13:57.834 --> 00:13:59.667
I would almost bristle at that advice,

00:13:59.701 --> 00:14:01.901
like, OK, if it's so easy,
why don't you do it?

00:14:01.934 --> 00:14:03.067
You know what I mean?

00:14:03.101 --> 00:14:05.901
How the heck am I supposed
to get from point A to point B?

00:14:05.901 --> 00:14:09.334
I don't actually know
how to change my reaction.

00:14:09.367 --> 00:14:10.967
And so in many ways,

00:14:10.967 --> 00:14:14.534
"The Other Side of Change" was a response
to this personal need I had,

00:14:14.567 --> 00:14:17.901
which is I'm convinced
that it would be beneficial for me

00:14:17.934 --> 00:14:20.567
to have a different reaction
to what I'm going through.

00:14:20.567 --> 00:14:24.267
But I don't know what
the science-based techniques are

00:14:24.301 --> 00:14:27.067
or what the right questions
I'm supposed to ask myself are,

00:14:27.067 --> 00:14:29.701
or what wisdom I can glean
from other people's stories.

00:14:29.701 --> 00:14:31.234
So like, please give that to me.

00:14:31.267 --> 00:14:34.234
And so that was sort of the goal
of "The Other Side of Change."

00:14:34.267 --> 00:14:36.734
And I think one of the overarching
messages of hope,

00:14:36.767 --> 00:14:39.167
outside of the Change Survival Kit,

00:14:39.167 --> 00:14:41.601
which I give to each reader
at the end of the book,

00:14:41.634 --> 00:14:43.901
which is a distillation
of all those strategies,

00:14:43.901 --> 00:14:45.101
is to remind people

00:14:45.134 --> 00:14:49.067
that when a big change happens to you,

00:14:49.067 --> 00:14:52.501
it also leads to lasting
change within you.

00:14:52.534 --> 00:14:55.701
We often forget
that we are works in progress,

00:14:55.701 --> 00:14:59.201
that we are constantly changing ourselves.

00:14:59.234 --> 00:15:03.034
And so when we're feeling really
daunted at the outset of a change,

00:15:03.067 --> 00:15:05.934
like, we can't possibly
navigate what's up ahead,

00:15:05.967 --> 00:15:08.401
we have to remind ourselves
that we are constrained

00:15:08.434 --> 00:15:11.367
by our current abilities
and values and perspectives,

00:15:11.401 --> 00:15:14.767
and that we can undergo
a huge transformation

00:15:14.801 --> 00:15:18.434
as a result of the big changes
that happen in our lives.

00:15:18.434 --> 00:15:22.034
And one, I think,

00:15:22.067 --> 00:15:25.234
universal message I heard
from the cast of characters

00:15:25.267 --> 00:15:27.601
that I interviewed
for "The Other Side of Change"

00:15:27.634 --> 00:15:31.267
is that while they weren't
necessarily grateful

00:15:31.301 --> 00:15:33.001
for the changes they went through,

00:15:33.034 --> 00:15:36.801
they were very hard,
like, illness and loss and heartbreak,

00:15:36.801 --> 00:15:39.901
they were deeply grateful
for the person they became

00:15:39.934 --> 00:15:42.667
as a result of what they went through.

00:15:42.967 --> 00:15:47.767
You know, the new environment
they were thrust into

00:15:47.801 --> 00:15:52.334
created this rich,
fertile environment for growth

00:15:52.367 --> 00:15:56.201
and allowed them to see themselves
in the world in really new ways

00:15:56.234 --> 00:15:58.401
that ultimately unlocked freedom

00:15:58.401 --> 00:16:01.167
or a new way of seeing themselves
or seeing their families

00:16:01.201 --> 00:16:04.101
or seeing their self-identities
that propelled them forward

00:16:04.134 --> 00:16:07.401
and led to a better version of themselves.

00:16:07.434 --> 00:16:10.434
And I'm curious to know
if you found something similar

00:16:10.434 --> 00:16:12.667
in all the research
that you did for the book.

00:16:12.701 --> 00:16:17.601
I mean, our books are similar in the focus
on both science and stories, right?

00:16:18.101 --> 00:16:19.734
And obviously,

00:16:19.734 --> 00:16:22.901
you spent a lot of time interviewing
people about their experiences

00:16:22.934 --> 00:16:24.667
of navigating uncertainty.

00:16:25.234 --> 00:16:27.167
SS: Well, I think one thing that I found

00:16:27.167 --> 00:16:31.601
is that when we are willing to turn
toward what we don't know,

00:16:31.601 --> 00:16:36.567
when we're willing to withstand
some of the stress and anxiety

00:16:36.567 --> 00:16:40.601
and fear that comes
from facing uncertainty,

00:16:40.634 --> 00:16:43.434
we can get to a place that is greater

00:16:43.467 --> 00:16:47.067
than what we could have otherwise
imagined before doing so.

00:16:47.101 --> 00:16:49.801
So one, just sort of example
from the business world.

00:16:49.834 --> 00:16:54.701
In the early 2010s, there was
this start-up called Tiny Speck,

00:16:54.734 --> 00:16:56.734
and it was sort of the belle of the ball.

00:16:56.734 --> 00:17:00.101
They built this massive
online multiplayer game called Glitch,

00:17:00.134 --> 00:17:03.734
and they had raised $17 million
before they had launched.

00:17:03.734 --> 00:17:06.234
And their launch was covered
in "The New York Times,"

00:17:06.234 --> 00:17:10.067
and they had tens of thousands
of active players from the get-go.

00:17:10.067 --> 00:17:14.001
And yet, the founder of the company
felt in his heart of hearts

00:17:14.034 --> 00:17:16.501
that they weren't on a sustainable path.

00:17:16.534 --> 00:17:19.434
There was something that wasn't
quite right with the business.

00:17:19.467 --> 00:17:23.734
And so he decided to do something
that other people thought was insane.

00:17:23.734 --> 00:17:26.501
Less than two years
into founding the company,

00:17:26.534 --> 00:17:30.867
he decided to shut the game down,
sort of at the peak of its success,

00:17:30.901 --> 00:17:33.001
and he offered to make
his investors whole.

00:17:33.034 --> 00:17:36.634
He gave employees that wanted
to leave the opportunity to leave,

00:17:36.667 --> 00:17:39.367
and then he decided
to pivot the organization

00:17:39.401 --> 00:17:42.234
around this internal-communications
tool that they had built

00:17:42.234 --> 00:17:45.134
so that they can collaborate
across different locations.

00:17:45.167 --> 00:17:47.767
And talking to the founder
at the time, he said,

00:17:47.801 --> 00:17:51.667
"At the moment, I didn't know
exactly what the possibility

00:17:51.701 --> 00:17:53.501
or the opportunity was.

00:17:53.534 --> 00:17:56.067
But through making this change,

00:17:56.101 --> 00:17:57.734
we were able to discover it."

00:17:58.267 --> 00:18:00.301
And that internal-communications tool

00:18:00.301 --> 00:18:03.667
is what you and I both
know today as Slack.

00:18:04.067 --> 00:18:07.067
And that founder's name
is Stewart Butterfield.

00:18:07.101 --> 00:18:12.001
And Slack became one of the most
successful software companies of all time.

00:18:12.301 --> 00:18:13.901
I think what that story shows

00:18:13.934 --> 00:18:19.167
is that when we are able
to turn towards the fog,

00:18:19.167 --> 00:18:22.234
when we were able to maybe
descend the peak

00:18:22.267 --> 00:18:25.167
that we thought we were standing
on the top of the world from,

00:18:25.201 --> 00:18:29.567
we can discover a higher peak
that's waiting just around the corner,

00:18:29.601 --> 00:18:32.867
or discover an element of ourselves,

00:18:32.901 --> 00:18:37.167
an opportunity, a possibility that comes
at the threshold of what we know.

00:18:37.201 --> 00:18:42.267
Nearly every scientific breakthrough
or genre-busting piece of art

00:18:42.301 --> 00:18:45.967
or generational company
began with someone's willingness

00:18:45.967 --> 00:18:48.067
to get to a point of uncertainty

00:18:48.101 --> 00:18:51.967
and then, rather than turn back
or turn towards the safe option

00:18:52.001 --> 00:18:54.134
or turn towards something
that was derivative

00:18:54.134 --> 00:18:55.967
of something that they've done before,

00:18:56.001 --> 00:18:57.201
they persisted.

00:18:57.234 --> 00:18:59.501
One of my favorite quotes
from "How to Not Know"

00:18:59.534 --> 00:19:01.534
is from this psychologist named Rollo May.

00:19:01.567 --> 00:19:03.767
And he says, "Commitment is healthiest,

00:19:03.801 --> 00:19:06.334
not in the absence of doubt,

00:19:06.334 --> 00:19:08.434
but in spite of doubt."

00:19:08.867 --> 00:19:13.501
And so if we're able to press forward,
make decisions, commit to things,

00:19:13.534 --> 00:19:15.567
even though we're not sure,

00:19:15.601 --> 00:19:19.367
we can discover possibilities
waiting on the other side.

00:19:21.334 --> 00:19:24.401
MS: For those of us who have
that gut-level discomfort

00:19:24.434 --> 00:19:26.267
with uncertainty, right,

00:19:26.301 --> 00:19:28.367
it sounds good to hear you say,

00:19:28.367 --> 00:19:31.401
oh, there's possibility
on the other side of uncertainty.

00:19:31.767 --> 00:19:35.801
But what is your advice
for how to actually embrace it?

00:19:35.801 --> 00:19:38.501
Because I think for many of us,

00:19:38.534 --> 00:19:40.601
it's very challenging

00:19:40.634 --> 00:19:46.267
to just overcome the physical feelings
of nausea that we have,

00:19:46.301 --> 00:19:48.434
when we are up against the unknown.

00:19:48.467 --> 00:19:51.834
SS: And even, you know,
the term "embrace uncertainty"

00:19:51.867 --> 00:19:54.901
can feel sort of
like gaslighting sometimes.

00:19:54.934 --> 00:19:56.401
MS: Or like a platitude.

00:19:56.434 --> 00:19:57.867
SS: Yeah, it's like, you know,

00:19:57.867 --> 00:20:00.101
I'm feeling incredibly scared
and uncomfortable

00:20:00.101 --> 00:20:01.867
and you're telling me to embrace it?

00:20:01.867 --> 00:20:04.234
Like, no, screw you,
I don't want to embrace that.

00:20:04.267 --> 00:20:07.601
But I think there's something
about understanding

00:20:07.601 --> 00:20:11.601
that uncertainty doesn't
necessarily mean a threat,

00:20:11.634 --> 00:20:15.834
even though that's the way
that our biology is wired.

00:20:15.867 --> 00:20:18.734
Uncertainty can also be
that birthplace of possibility.

00:20:18.767 --> 00:20:23.967
So one thing I often tell people is,
step one is to find your anchors.

00:20:23.967 --> 00:20:27.267
When we are certain
about some elements of our life,

00:20:27.301 --> 00:20:30.967
it makes it easier to hold
uncertainty in others.

00:20:31.001 --> 00:20:34.334
So Maya, for you, for example,
that knowledge about your why,

00:20:34.367 --> 00:20:37.401
that knowledge about wanting to connect

00:20:37.401 --> 00:20:40.234
is something that will be steady

00:20:40.267 --> 00:20:43.067
amidst all of the change of your future.

00:20:43.401 --> 00:20:48.334
Maybe that anchor for you is a commitment
to live in a particular place,

00:20:48.367 --> 00:20:50.301
or a commitment to a spouse,

00:20:50.334 --> 00:20:53.167
or commitment to a set of values.

00:20:53.201 --> 00:20:56.401
But when we can get clear
on those anchors,

00:20:56.434 --> 00:21:00.434
it makes it easier to hold uncertainty
in other realms of our life.

00:21:01.667 --> 00:21:03.467
The other thing that I'll say

00:21:03.467 --> 00:21:07.201
is that you have to have
some element of faith.

00:21:07.834 --> 00:21:11.201
I don't mean this necessarily
in a religious or dogmatic sense,

00:21:11.234 --> 00:21:14.367
but I used to work at this design firm
Ideo, as I mentioned,

00:21:14.401 --> 00:21:18.467
and we used to have this metaphor
which is facing uncertainty,

00:21:18.501 --> 00:21:23.834
being a leader in spite of uncertainty
feels like you're on a boat

00:21:23.867 --> 00:21:27.101
on a lake that's shrouded
in really heavy fog.

00:21:27.901 --> 00:21:30.834
You might not be able
to see very far in front of you

00:21:30.867 --> 00:21:33.767
or know exactly where you'll end up.

00:21:34.267 --> 00:21:35.467
But you have two jobs.

00:21:35.467 --> 00:21:39.167
One is to maintain faith
that you'll eventually reach land.

00:21:39.201 --> 00:21:40.734
Remember, you're on a lake.

00:21:41.134 --> 00:21:43.334
And the second is to keep rowing.

00:21:44.067 --> 00:21:46.734
And I think that second part is the key.

00:21:46.767 --> 00:21:48.934
Is that through action,

00:21:48.967 --> 00:21:51.267
clarity can begin to emerge.

00:21:51.601 --> 00:21:54.234
Because I think one
of the most paralyzing things

00:21:54.267 --> 00:21:57.001
about change or transition

00:21:57.001 --> 00:21:59.167
or uncertainty or doubt

00:21:59.201 --> 00:22:01.767
is that it keeps us stuck.

00:22:01.801 --> 00:22:05.834
It keeps us not wanting to leave
the house or go outside

00:22:05.867 --> 00:22:07.334
or reach out to a friend.

00:22:07.767 --> 00:22:11.167
But that action can absorb our anxiety.

00:22:11.567 --> 00:22:15.467
I wrote a chapter about dealing
with climate uncertainty,

00:22:15.501 --> 00:22:20.234
dealing with the sort of foreboding
cloud on the horizon.

00:22:20.701 --> 00:22:23.301
And I spoke to this psychologist

00:22:23.301 --> 00:22:26.834
who specializes in helping people
deal with climate anxiety.

00:22:27.301 --> 00:22:31.467
And she said the same thing
that will help your anxiety

00:22:31.501 --> 00:22:33.001
is the thing that we need

00:22:33.034 --> 00:22:35.534
to make a difference
in the climate crisis,

00:22:35.567 --> 00:22:39.234
which is to think about this tapestry
of this huge problem

00:22:39.267 --> 00:22:41.834
and find that one string to pull.

00:22:42.401 --> 00:22:45.234
Find that one action that you can take.

00:22:45.601 --> 00:22:49.634
And that's where you can find
a sense of agency, of autonomy

00:22:49.667 --> 00:22:52.534
to feel like you're moving
toward a solution.

00:22:52.901 --> 00:22:54.901
Because I think one thing that is so hard

00:22:54.934 --> 00:22:57.667
when people are in the midst
of this change

00:22:57.701 --> 00:23:00.101
of knowing where to put their feet,

00:23:00.134 --> 00:23:01.934
where to direct their energy.

00:23:02.301 --> 00:23:04.167
And in the words of Martin Luther King,

00:23:04.201 --> 00:23:08.267
you can take one step and hopefully
the staircase will emerge.

00:23:09.167 --> 00:23:14.801
MS: One of the most meaningful
stories that I wrote about in the book

00:23:14.834 --> 00:23:17.267
concerns a guy named
Reginald Dwayne Betts.

00:23:17.301 --> 00:23:18.667
SS: I love this story.

00:23:19.334 --> 00:23:23.067
MS: He was sentenced
to nine years in adult prison

00:23:23.101 --> 00:23:26.267
as a 16-year-old
for a carjacking he committed.

00:23:26.301 --> 00:23:29.601
And Dwayne had been
a very promising student.

00:23:29.601 --> 00:23:31.834
He was class treasurer,

00:23:31.834 --> 00:23:35.401
he had his sights set
on going to Georgia Tech,

00:23:35.401 --> 00:23:37.867
he had been ID'd
as talented and gifted,

00:23:37.901 --> 00:23:40.534
he was a wonderfully loving
and devoted son to his mom.

00:23:40.567 --> 00:23:42.301
He had so much promise.

00:23:42.701 --> 00:23:46.567
And in a moment, in an effort
to try to prove his machismo

00:23:46.601 --> 00:23:49.267
to the boys in his neighborhood,
to prove his toughness,

00:23:49.267 --> 00:23:51.134
he did this terrible thing

00:23:51.167 --> 00:23:55.334
and then got this incredibly
long prison sentence as a result.

00:23:55.701 --> 00:23:59.967
And what was so fascinating for me,

00:23:59.967 --> 00:24:01.834
in writing "The other Side of Change,"

00:24:01.834 --> 00:24:04.601
was to probe people's interior lives

00:24:04.601 --> 00:24:07.001
and figure out what it was
about the change

00:24:07.034 --> 00:24:09.067
that was scariest for them.

00:24:09.101 --> 00:24:12.967
And what was so interesting
is Dwayne told me,

00:24:13.001 --> 00:24:17.301
"You know, I certainly was grieving
the life that I could no longer live

00:24:17.334 --> 00:24:19.067
as a result of this prison sentence,

00:24:19.101 --> 00:24:21.701
like going to prom
and graduating with my friends

00:24:21.701 --> 00:24:23.934
and studying at college,

00:24:23.967 --> 00:24:25.601
studying engineering.

00:24:25.634 --> 00:24:29.634
But I was actually as afraid,

00:24:29.634 --> 00:24:33.667
I was so concerned
about who I might become

00:24:33.667 --> 00:24:35.567
now that I was behind bars."

00:24:36.401 --> 00:24:38.001
That cut through me like a knife,

00:24:38.034 --> 00:24:39.667
I was like, wow.

00:24:40.467 --> 00:24:44.201
It actually speaks to a concept
in psychology called possible selves.

00:24:44.234 --> 00:24:47.667
So we have all these possible selves
that we construct each and every day

00:24:47.701 --> 00:24:49.034
just by virtue of living.

00:24:49.367 --> 00:24:50.801
So we have expected selves

00:24:50.834 --> 00:24:52.201
which are the versions of us

00:24:52.201 --> 00:24:54.934
that we just think are most likely
to happen, good or bad.

00:24:54.967 --> 00:24:58.434
We have hoped-for selves which reflect
our dreams and aspirations,

00:24:58.434 --> 00:25:02.601
and then we have feared selves,
which reflect our anxieties and worries

00:25:02.634 --> 00:25:03.901
about who we might become.

00:25:03.934 --> 00:25:07.501
And Dwayne felt possessed
by that latter bucket, right?

00:25:07.501 --> 00:25:11.101
He was now feeling like
all these positive selves --

00:25:11.101 --> 00:25:12.967
those doors were slammed shut,

00:25:12.967 --> 00:25:17.901
and now all these doors were ajar open
that he didn't want to see open, right?

00:25:17.934 --> 00:25:21.601
Like, will I develop a gambling
habit while I'm in prison?

00:25:21.634 --> 00:25:25.301
Will I have to become violent
in order to protect myself?

00:25:25.834 --> 00:25:27.467
Will I get addicted to cigarettes?

00:25:27.501 --> 00:25:30.234
Like, he had all these concerns
about who he might become.

00:25:31.734 --> 00:25:37.134
The mechanism that helped
unlock this brighter future for him

00:25:37.167 --> 00:25:39.501
is called moral elevation.

00:25:40.467 --> 00:25:43.934
Moral elevation is this
warm, fuzzy feeling

00:25:43.967 --> 00:25:45.234
that we get in our chest

00:25:45.234 --> 00:25:47.801
when we witness someone else's
extraordinary behavior.

00:25:47.801 --> 00:25:51.367
So that could be their kindness
or courage or self-sacrifice.

00:25:52.267 --> 00:25:54.534
Whatever the extraordinary trait,

00:25:54.567 --> 00:25:57.567
interestingly, moral elevation
doesn't simply feel good.

00:25:57.567 --> 00:26:00.167
It doesn't just restore
our faith in humanity.

00:26:00.201 --> 00:26:02.334
It actually rewires our brains.

00:26:02.367 --> 00:26:04.967
And that's because when
we witness someone else

00:26:05.001 --> 00:26:09.634
engage in behaviors that violate
our understanding of what humans are like,

00:26:09.667 --> 00:26:13.367
what humans are capable of
in the best way possible,

00:26:13.367 --> 00:26:16.267
it actually cracks open
our own imagination

00:26:16.301 --> 00:26:18.234
about what we are capable of.

00:26:19.001 --> 00:26:20.634
And for Dwayne,

00:26:20.667 --> 00:26:23.301
this manifested in the form of --

00:26:23.334 --> 00:26:24.901
this moral elevation manifested

00:26:24.901 --> 00:26:28.767
in the form of a fellow
prisoner named Bilal,

00:26:28.767 --> 00:26:31.634
who he came across about one year
into his prison sentence.

00:26:31.667 --> 00:26:33.234
And for Dwayne,

00:26:33.267 --> 00:26:36.667
Bilal violated all of the stereotypes
Dwayne had in his head

00:26:36.701 --> 00:26:39.567
of what it meant
to carry the label of prisoner.

00:26:40.134 --> 00:26:43.034
So in Dwayne's mind, you had
to be ruthlessly self-interested,

00:26:43.067 --> 00:26:45.267
you have to keep your cards
close to the chest,

00:26:45.301 --> 00:26:48.201
you should never build friendships
or get close to anyone else

00:26:48.234 --> 00:26:51.901
or it could be jeopardizing to your health
and well-being and overall safety.

00:26:51.934 --> 00:26:56.701
But Bilal was the kind of guy
who made it a point to stand out.

00:26:56.734 --> 00:27:00.167
His prison uniform
was crisply ironed every day,

00:27:00.201 --> 00:27:01.967
and he was clean shaven,

00:27:02.001 --> 00:27:06.167
and he would get up two hours
before count time to do 250 push-ups.

00:27:06.201 --> 00:27:08.801
And he spent his free time

00:27:08.834 --> 00:27:10.801
coaching the younger men on how to box

00:27:10.801 --> 00:27:14.601
so that they could protect themselves
from the threat of violence within prison.

00:27:14.634 --> 00:27:17.067
And so Dwayne would see him
teaching these young men

00:27:17.101 --> 00:27:18.901
how to box in the prison yard.

00:27:18.934 --> 00:27:23.967
And Dwayne says that Bilal
was kind of taking this stance,

00:27:24.001 --> 00:27:28.267
like, "No, this is who I'm choosing
to be as a prisoner."

00:27:28.801 --> 00:27:31.834
And it was a radical experience for Dwayne

00:27:31.867 --> 00:27:34.001
because he, for the first time, realized

00:27:34.034 --> 00:27:37.367
that perhaps he wasn't destined

00:27:37.367 --> 00:27:38.967
to become a certain person,

00:27:39.001 --> 00:27:42.334
to have a certain future
just because he was a prisoner.

00:27:42.701 --> 00:27:45.201
And it was with that
more empowered mindset

00:27:45.234 --> 00:27:47.567
that when he stumbled
upon a book of poetry

00:27:47.601 --> 00:27:50.567
while in solitary confinement
a few weeks later,

00:27:50.601 --> 00:27:54.467
and read a poem about a young Black man

00:27:54.501 --> 00:27:56.701
who had experienced
sexual violence in prison,

00:27:56.701 --> 00:28:00.601
a 14 or 15-year-old,
in this poem, he realized,

00:28:00.601 --> 00:28:04.534
"Well, you know, I can't do
for the young men here

00:28:04.567 --> 00:28:05.734
what Bilal's doing,

00:28:05.767 --> 00:28:08.267
I'm not strong, I don't know how to box.

00:28:08.667 --> 00:28:12.234
But what I can do
is dignify their experiences

00:28:12.267 --> 00:28:13.834
through the written word."

00:28:13.867 --> 00:28:16.634
Like, "I know how to write,
I can do this."

00:28:16.667 --> 00:28:20.101
And he started to obsessively
write poems, Simone.

00:28:20.134 --> 00:28:21.634
Like, day after day after day.

00:28:21.667 --> 00:28:23.734
And when he would write
a sufficient number,

00:28:23.767 --> 00:28:27.967
he would bind them together using
thread from his pillowcase

00:28:28.001 --> 00:28:30.301
and you create a little book, you know.

00:28:30.334 --> 00:28:34.601
And I'll save the details for anyone
who reads "The Other Side of Change,"

00:28:34.634 --> 00:28:37.734
because Dwayne will always say
his story better than I will.

00:28:38.267 --> 00:28:42.534
But fast forward and today,
Dwayne is a Yale Law School graduate,

00:28:42.534 --> 00:28:44.567
he's a MacArthur Genius prize winner.

00:28:44.601 --> 00:28:47.734
He writes some of the most
stirring poetry I've ever heard

00:28:47.767 --> 00:28:49.867
on the topic of race and prison.

00:28:49.901 --> 00:28:54.401
And what's so beautiful to me
about his story is that, you know,

00:28:54.434 --> 00:28:58.167
you talked earlier about how uncertainty
can be so constraining.

00:28:58.467 --> 00:29:02.601
And that's the same for unexpected
negative changes, right?

00:29:02.634 --> 00:29:07.034
We impose our own mental filter
onto that situation and we think,

00:29:07.067 --> 00:29:09.734
well, I have some expectations,

00:29:09.767 --> 00:29:14.201
some presumptions about what it means
to be a prisoner or a widow

00:29:14.234 --> 00:29:17.601
or someone who's unemployed
or someone who's chronically ill.

00:29:17.634 --> 00:29:20.267
And we have a very limited imagination

00:29:20.301 --> 00:29:22.334
as a result about what that can look like.

00:29:22.367 --> 00:29:28.334
And I love the idea that moral elevation
is actually available to all of us.

00:29:28.367 --> 00:29:30.901
It's a form of awe in our everyday life.

00:29:30.901 --> 00:29:35.167
As long as we're willing to be present
and to receive that inspiration.

00:29:36.034 --> 00:29:40.634
And when we feel that doors are closing,

00:29:40.667 --> 00:29:44.201
taking a walk and putting our phone away
and just observing how people are.

00:29:44.901 --> 00:29:46.167
Oh, wow, that was so nice.

00:29:46.167 --> 00:29:48.734
The way that person was so kind
to that elderly woman.

00:29:48.767 --> 00:29:51.667
Or you're witnessing, say,
your kids on the playground.

00:29:51.701 --> 00:29:54.801
Oh my God, look at the way
that little kid stood up to the bully.

00:29:54.834 --> 00:29:56.934
Like that's another form
of moral elevation.

00:29:56.967 --> 00:29:58.834
It's everywhere we look.

00:29:59.301 --> 00:30:01.367
It's so beautiful and empowering

00:30:01.401 --> 00:30:04.701
because we know of its ability
to rewire the brain.

00:30:04.734 --> 00:30:07.101
But I was thinking,
based on the comment you made

00:30:07.134 --> 00:30:09.201
and the MLK quote,

00:30:09.201 --> 00:30:11.401
that it's actually just as important

00:30:11.434 --> 00:30:16.634
that we be a moment
of moral elevation for someone else,

00:30:16.634 --> 00:30:20.334
that we be the moral beauty
that can inspire other people.

00:30:20.367 --> 00:30:22.034
And I think that can be, you know,

00:30:22.067 --> 00:30:24.801
so many of us are feeling helpless
in this moment in time.

00:30:24.834 --> 00:30:27.101
Like, can I change the world at all?

00:30:27.134 --> 00:30:28.401
Do I matter at all?

00:30:28.434 --> 00:30:30.301
Is there anything I can do

00:30:30.334 --> 00:30:33.467
in this moment of total
disconnection and seeming chaos?

00:30:33.867 --> 00:30:37.301
And by being our best selves actually,

00:30:37.301 --> 00:30:41.834
we can have these beautiful effects
on other human beings

00:30:41.834 --> 00:30:44.934
in ways that we might never even perceive,
but they're happening.

00:30:44.967 --> 00:30:46.201
SS: Yeah, I love that.

00:30:46.201 --> 00:30:49.067
And I think we can also think
about moral elevation

00:30:49.101 --> 00:30:51.534
in the context of our past lives as well,

00:30:51.567 --> 00:30:55.334
and thinking about moments
in our past where we have stood up,

00:30:55.367 --> 00:30:57.434
where we have been that aspirational self

00:30:57.467 --> 00:30:59.667
that we wanted to be
in the future as well.

00:30:59.701 --> 00:31:01.834
I think it's one of the best
things we can do

00:31:01.834 --> 00:31:03.734
to build our tolerance for uncertainty,

00:31:03.767 --> 00:31:04.934
is to remind ourselves

00:31:04.967 --> 00:31:08.801
that we have likely been through
uncertain moments before.

00:31:08.834 --> 00:31:12.134
We have navigated change and transition,

00:31:12.167 --> 00:31:16.567
and moments where we think we don't
know exactly what the future will hold.

00:31:16.601 --> 00:31:19.534
And maybe that can help
build the case, the conviction,

00:31:19.567 --> 00:31:22.234
the evidence that we can
continue to do so again.

00:31:22.801 --> 00:31:25.567
So maybe let's transition
into some questions from members.

00:31:25.601 --> 00:31:27.701
There's so many good ones here.

00:31:27.701 --> 00:31:29.967
Do you want to ask
this first one from Michelle?

00:31:30.001 --> 00:31:31.867
MS: Yes, I'll ask this one for you.

00:31:32.167 --> 00:31:33.701
From Michelle M,

00:31:33.734 --> 00:31:35.834
"In a world that demands answers,

00:31:35.867 --> 00:31:38.067
what's the one question
we can ask ourselves

00:31:38.101 --> 00:31:40.601
in the exact moment we don't know,

00:31:40.634 --> 00:31:43.734
when uncertainty feels
physically uncomfortable

00:31:43.734 --> 00:31:48.434
that reliably turns that discomfort
into curiosity and a clear next step?"

00:31:48.867 --> 00:31:50.034
Oh, I love this so much.

00:31:50.067 --> 00:31:51.434
SS: Yeah, deep question.

00:31:51.967 --> 00:31:54.401
A brief aside before
I answer it more directly,

00:31:54.434 --> 00:31:58.534
which is, I spoke
to this ethicist for the book,

00:31:58.567 --> 00:32:00.801
and I was asking him specifically

00:32:00.834 --> 00:32:03.667
about how to make decisions
when we don't know.

00:32:03.701 --> 00:32:07.934
And his name is Ira Bedzow,
and he works at Emory University.

00:32:07.934 --> 00:32:11.767
He both works as a rabbi,
an ethicist in a hospital

00:32:11.801 --> 00:32:13.234
and as a college counselor.

00:32:13.234 --> 00:32:17.701
So he's constantly advising people
through these murky, foggy situations.

00:32:17.734 --> 00:32:20.334
And he says, when he works
with students in particular,

00:32:20.367 --> 00:32:23.867
he always asks them the same
three questions when they're unsure.

00:32:24.634 --> 00:32:26.501
The first is what do you want?

00:32:27.067 --> 00:32:30.467
And the idea here is to try
and get out of our thinking brain

00:32:30.501 --> 00:32:32.767
and more into sort of our embodied wisdom.

00:32:32.767 --> 00:32:34.467
What does your gut tell you?

00:32:35.401 --> 00:32:38.334
The second is, do you want to want that,

00:32:38.367 --> 00:32:39.667
which is an attempt to get

00:32:39.701 --> 00:32:43.467
into what philosophers sometimes call
our second-order selves.

00:32:43.501 --> 00:32:46.167
So our reflection on our ego.

00:32:46.501 --> 00:32:47.867
Do I want a cigarette?

00:32:47.901 --> 00:32:49.101
Maybe yes.

00:32:49.101 --> 00:32:50.567
Do I want to want a cigarette?

00:32:50.567 --> 00:32:51.734
Maybe no.

00:32:51.767 --> 00:32:55.034
But the third question is the one
that I think is the most important,

00:32:55.034 --> 00:32:59.134
which is what does this choice say
about who you are as a person?

00:32:59.734 --> 00:33:05.334
And I think this is maybe the best
answer I have to Michelle's question.

00:33:05.367 --> 00:33:07.634
When you're in a moment of uncertainty,

00:33:07.667 --> 00:33:10.401
when you're having to decide
between multiple paths,

00:33:10.434 --> 00:33:14.267
you can't know exactly
how each of them will turn out.

00:33:14.667 --> 00:33:18.534
But what you can do is try
to connect your choice,

00:33:18.567 --> 00:33:23.101
your decision to your values
and your identity.

00:33:23.501 --> 00:33:26.334
And then if you're able to make a decision

00:33:26.367 --> 00:33:28.701
that's in alignment with your values,

00:33:28.701 --> 00:33:31.267
hopefully you can stand by that decision

00:33:31.267 --> 00:33:35.001
even if you don't get
the outcome that you desire.

00:33:35.467 --> 00:33:37.334
And so that's what I think a lot about

00:33:37.334 --> 00:33:40.501
in terms of what to do
when we're at these crossroads in life.

00:33:40.534 --> 00:33:42.634
And the moment before we make a decision,

00:33:42.667 --> 00:33:43.901
sometimes it's so hard

00:33:43.934 --> 00:33:46.901
because all of these possibilities
are available to us,

00:33:46.934 --> 00:33:49.867
and therefore, making any choice
feels like foreclosing

00:33:49.901 --> 00:33:51.467
on all these other choices.

00:33:51.467 --> 00:33:53.867
It can feel like a loss more than a gain.

00:33:53.901 --> 00:33:57.501
But if we're able to connect our decision
to who we want to be,

00:33:57.501 --> 00:34:01.234
to that aspirational self,
to our identities and values,

00:34:01.267 --> 00:34:03.267
then hopefully we can stand by them.

00:34:03.967 --> 00:34:05.201
Thanks for that question.

00:34:05.234 --> 00:34:06.634
OK, I'll ask one to Maya.

00:34:07.201 --> 00:34:08.767
This is from Kim K.

00:34:08.801 --> 00:34:10.001
She says,

00:34:10.034 --> 00:34:13.734
"The Chinese symbol for chaos
is also the symbol for opportunity.

00:34:13.767 --> 00:34:15.134
Care to elucidate?"

00:34:16.567 --> 00:34:18.701
MS: Yeah, I was obsessed
with this question,

00:34:18.734 --> 00:34:21.567
in part because my husband
is Chinese American.

00:34:21.601 --> 00:34:25.167
And so I spent years trying
to learn Mandarin.

00:34:26.001 --> 00:34:28.534
And so, yeah, let's just say

00:34:28.567 --> 00:34:30.434
my speaking abilities are much greater

00:34:30.467 --> 00:34:33.501
than the writing of characters abilities.

00:34:35.467 --> 00:34:39.167
One thing that was so interesting
because I actually think

00:34:39.201 --> 00:34:41.401
this speaks to what Kim has asked about,

00:34:41.401 --> 00:34:46.367
that I learned in researching the book
is that the word apocalypse,

00:34:46.401 --> 00:34:48.567
which is often what we feel
is happening to us

00:34:48.567 --> 00:34:50.601
in the throes of a massive change, right?

00:34:50.601 --> 00:34:54.334
It's like I'm in a personal apocalypse
where I no longer have access

00:34:54.367 --> 00:34:55.534
to this prior world,

00:34:55.567 --> 00:34:59.601
the world that I am currently
living in is a foreign one, right?

00:35:01.067 --> 00:35:06.301
The word apocalypse comes
from the Greek word apocalypsis,

00:35:06.334 --> 00:35:08.401
which actually means revelation.

00:35:08.701 --> 00:35:12.301
And I find that etymology so instructive.

00:35:12.334 --> 00:35:14.534
Because yes, change can upend us,

00:35:14.567 --> 00:35:17.201
but it can also reveal things to us

00:35:17.234 --> 00:35:19.701
about who we are and what our values are

00:35:19.734 --> 00:35:22.934
and what our perspectives are
and what our abilities are.

00:35:22.967 --> 00:35:27.401
And that revelation brings
into the light parts of us,

00:35:27.401 --> 00:35:30.234
parts of who we are that were
previously hidden from sight.

00:35:30.267 --> 00:35:33.134
I think it's very easy for us
to believe, in everyday life,

00:35:33.167 --> 00:35:36.267
that we have a fairly good command
of who we are as human beings,

00:35:36.301 --> 00:35:40.201
but the reality is that we've only
had access to the parts of us

00:35:40.234 --> 00:35:44.234
that were revealed
to the world, revealed to us,

00:35:44.267 --> 00:35:47.901
in response to the seemingly random
set of life experiences we've had.

00:35:47.934 --> 00:35:51.667
It's a set of data points
that aren't comprehensive.

00:35:52.034 --> 00:35:55.001
And so one thing
that's beautiful about change

00:35:55.034 --> 00:36:01.034
is that in creating tension
or creating pressure in some way,

00:36:01.034 --> 00:36:02.434
you start to see parts of you

00:36:02.467 --> 00:36:05.034
that you just didn't
ever think to evaluate

00:36:05.067 --> 00:36:08.067
or investigate or even really
acknowledge in the first place.

00:36:08.101 --> 00:36:14.301
And Simo, I know both of us end
our books on more personal notes, right?

00:36:14.334 --> 00:36:17.434
I didn't expect for the last
chapter of my book

00:36:17.434 --> 00:36:20.667
to be more memoir than anything else,

00:36:20.701 --> 00:36:23.867
because I had spent the earlier chapters
interviewing other people.

00:36:23.901 --> 00:36:25.501
But as I was writing the book,

00:36:25.534 --> 00:36:28.534
I was navigating these own changes
and losses in my own life.

00:36:28.567 --> 00:36:29.867
I alluded to this earlier,

00:36:29.901 --> 00:36:34.201
but my husband and I were not having
success in starting a family.

00:36:34.234 --> 00:36:37.101
And one of the many things

00:36:37.134 --> 00:36:42.767
that these experiences of loss
and despair revealed to me

00:36:42.767 --> 00:36:47.967
was I had placed a lot
of my self-worth as a woman

00:36:47.967 --> 00:36:50.634
in having children.

00:36:51.101 --> 00:36:53.801
And it might have been
from cultural influences.

00:36:53.801 --> 00:36:56.667
I think society also tells us

00:36:56.701 --> 00:36:59.701
that can be our primary
value in this world

00:36:59.701 --> 00:37:04.001
and in revealing a belief that I had,

00:37:04.034 --> 00:37:05.567
I was able to realize like,

00:37:05.601 --> 00:37:07.434
hey, just because I have this belief,

00:37:07.434 --> 00:37:10.701
doesn't mean that it was developed
on this sturdy foundation

00:37:10.734 --> 00:37:12.267
of thoughtful reasoning, right?

00:37:12.301 --> 00:37:15.434
Most of the beliefs we have,
we just inherited

00:37:15.467 --> 00:37:21.001
through you know, teachers
and messages from pop culture.

00:37:21.001 --> 00:37:24.667
And a lot of them entered subconsciously
without our even realizing it.

00:37:24.701 --> 00:37:28.834
And we never thought to interrogate
them or challenge those beliefs, right?

00:37:28.867 --> 00:37:32.301
And it's not like any of us have time
to wake up every day and say,

00:37:32.334 --> 00:37:34.767
hmm, let me think from the, you know,

00:37:34.801 --> 00:37:37.034
hundreds of thousands
of beliefs that I carry,

00:37:37.067 --> 00:37:38.901
which one should I investigate today?

00:37:38.934 --> 00:37:41.634
Like, that's not reasonable
to actually do.

00:37:41.667 --> 00:37:43.201
And it's uncomfortable as well,

00:37:43.201 --> 00:37:45.567
because a lot of times,
our beliefs are entangled

00:37:45.601 --> 00:37:48.267
with our sense of self-identity, right?

00:37:48.301 --> 00:37:50.334
So it can be a very uncomfortable process.

00:37:50.367 --> 00:37:52.534
And so for me,

00:37:52.567 --> 00:37:55.134
change in serving
as a moment of revelation

00:37:55.167 --> 00:37:58.534
also gives you a chance to revisit
the story you're telling yourself

00:37:58.567 --> 00:38:00.734
about who you are and where your worth is,

00:38:00.767 --> 00:38:02.467
where your worth is coming from.

00:38:02.467 --> 00:38:07.434
And I had to do a lot of unwinding
in that process of like,

00:38:07.434 --> 00:38:09.734
why do I feel that in
the absence of children,

00:38:09.734 --> 00:38:12.434
my life would be stripped
of all of its color and meaning

00:38:12.467 --> 00:38:16.001
and purpose and that my value
as a human being would be less?

00:38:16.001 --> 00:38:17.934
And slowly work through all that stuff.

00:38:17.967 --> 00:38:21.534
And, you know, as I write
at the end of the chapter, you know,

00:38:21.567 --> 00:38:23.301
in this moment I'm child-free

00:38:23.301 --> 00:38:26.801
and kind of unexpectedly,
I am the happiest,

00:38:26.801 --> 00:38:31.234
most hopeful, most grateful person.

00:38:31.267 --> 00:38:33.501
And I never saw that coming.

00:38:33.534 --> 00:38:36.501
You know, we talked earlier
about how change can transform us,

00:38:36.501 --> 00:38:39.001
but I wouldn't have believed that message.

00:38:39.034 --> 00:38:41.201
I would have just
thought of it as a platitude

00:38:41.234 --> 00:38:44.167
unless I'd felt that similar
transformation within me.

00:38:44.201 --> 00:38:47.634
Because if you had asked me, you know,

00:38:47.667 --> 00:38:50.767
on the night where we found out
we had lost identical twin girls,

00:38:50.767 --> 00:38:54.167
I would have been like, nope,
nothing positive will ever come from this.

00:38:54.201 --> 00:38:56.967
This is like a non-redemptive story.

00:38:57.434 --> 00:39:01.034
And yet it inspired so much
thinking and reflection

00:39:01.067 --> 00:39:03.867
and growth within me
that I couldn't have anticipated.

00:39:04.401 --> 00:39:07.267
And so all of this is to say like,

00:39:07.267 --> 00:39:11.601
I love Kim's question
because chaos, opportunity, right?

00:39:11.634 --> 00:39:15.534
Revelation, upheaval,
like, things are complicated.

00:39:15.534 --> 00:39:17.067
Experiences are complicated.

00:39:17.101 --> 00:39:18.767
They are rarely just good or bad.

00:39:18.801 --> 00:39:20.201
They're often multifold.

00:39:20.234 --> 00:39:23.734
And, if we are discerning enough

00:39:23.767 --> 00:39:27.867
to look for the opportunity
that sits within the rubble,

00:39:27.901 --> 00:39:29.934
so much can be unlocked.

00:39:30.334 --> 00:39:33.534
And I want to go to the next question,
but I also just want to hear

00:39:33.534 --> 00:39:35.267
any reflections you have --

00:39:35.301 --> 00:39:37.067
and by the way, Simo is his nickname,

00:39:37.067 --> 00:39:39.367
so I'm going by that on occasion,

00:39:39.401 --> 00:39:41.901
I realize I'm flipping back and forth --

00:39:41.934 --> 00:39:47.434
in which you talk about the uncertainty
that came alongside your journey

00:39:47.434 --> 00:39:49.001
into becoming parents.

00:39:49.734 --> 00:39:52.734
SS: Yeah, I think, you know,
pregnancy journeys

00:39:52.767 --> 00:39:58.134
are one of the greatest uncertainties
that a lot of people face in their lives.

00:39:58.167 --> 00:40:00.067
And similarly, you know,

00:40:00.101 --> 00:40:03.367
my wife and I were trying
to conceive for a long time

00:40:03.401 --> 00:40:05.501
and we got the news that we were pregnant.

00:40:05.501 --> 00:40:08.434
We were so excited,
we had the little app that said,

00:40:08.467 --> 00:40:10.934
"Your baby is the size of a poppy seed."

00:40:10.967 --> 00:40:14.334
And we started calling
this unborn child Poppy.

00:40:14.367 --> 00:40:16.767
And I remember going
to these doctor's appointments

00:40:16.801 --> 00:40:18.201
and just feeling so excited.

00:40:18.201 --> 00:40:22.934
And I had this whole life that I had
canvassed out for this unborn kid.

00:40:22.967 --> 00:40:25.367
And we went in for a doctor's appointment

00:40:25.401 --> 00:40:28.834
about three months into the pregnancy.

00:40:28.867 --> 00:40:32.001
And our doctor said,

00:40:32.034 --> 00:40:34.234
"At this stage, we'd expect
to see a heartbeat.

00:40:34.267 --> 00:40:36.467
And I don't see one right now.

00:40:36.501 --> 00:40:41.067
We're not yet ready to declare
this a lost pregnancy.

00:40:41.067 --> 00:40:45.701
Why don't you come back in a week
and we can do some more tests?"

00:40:46.201 --> 00:40:50.601
And meanwhile, I'm writing this book
about how to not know and uncertainty,

00:40:50.634 --> 00:40:52.934
and therefore it's coming
to the fore in my life.

00:40:52.934 --> 00:40:55.701
And I remember thinking
about that electric shock study

00:40:55.734 --> 00:40:57.567
and being like, just shock me already,

00:40:57.601 --> 00:40:59.434
you know, say the quiet part out loud.

00:40:59.467 --> 00:41:02.867
But in addition to the sort of
anticipatory grief,

00:41:02.901 --> 00:41:06.334
we also had to deal
with the waiting of the not knowing.

00:41:06.367 --> 00:41:08.801
And, you know, the week was really hard.

00:41:08.834 --> 00:41:12.834
I was just writing about that study
about breast cancer patients,

00:41:12.867 --> 00:41:15.601
where the waiting
before you get the diagnosis

00:41:15.634 --> 00:41:17.501
can be the most difficult part.

00:41:17.534 --> 00:41:21.634
But there were other elements of that week
that I think were really beautiful.

00:41:22.134 --> 00:41:26.767
My wife and I held eye contact
in crowded rooms for a little bit longer.

00:41:26.801 --> 00:41:29.667
We would, you know, maybe hug
for a little bit longer

00:41:29.701 --> 00:41:31.101
than we might have otherwise.

00:41:31.101 --> 00:41:34.067
And we kept repeating
this mantra to ourselves,

00:41:34.101 --> 00:41:35.934
which is, we are open,

00:41:35.934 --> 00:41:37.901
we are open, we are open.

00:41:38.634 --> 00:41:41.501
And a week later, we come
back to the doctor's office

00:41:41.534 --> 00:41:43.734
and we got the news that we expected.

00:41:43.734 --> 00:41:46.267
You know, we had lost Poppy.

00:41:46.301 --> 00:41:48.967
And it was incredibly sad.

00:41:50.301 --> 00:41:52.401
But I think one of the things

00:41:52.434 --> 00:41:54.534
that grief and loss can teach us

00:41:54.567 --> 00:41:57.034
is what we care about.

00:41:57.501 --> 00:42:01.501
And I think we can hurt
in proportion with how much we care.

00:42:02.101 --> 00:42:05.467
And long story short,

00:42:05.501 --> 00:42:06.667
a few months later,

00:42:06.701 --> 00:42:09.434
we were able to get pregnant again.

00:42:09.467 --> 00:42:13.034
And now I have a one-year-old named Luca.

00:42:13.567 --> 00:42:14.767
MS: And he's so cute.

00:42:14.801 --> 00:42:15.967
SS: He's very cute.

00:42:15.967 --> 00:42:18.567
I also think I wouldn't
appreciate it quite as much

00:42:18.601 --> 00:42:21.134
if not having gone through this loss.

00:42:21.167 --> 00:42:23.301
I think there's something
that you said, too,

00:42:23.334 --> 00:42:29.067
about not knowing exactly
how this loss in your case,

00:42:29.101 --> 00:42:30.667
would impact your ability

00:42:30.667 --> 00:42:33.434
to be grateful and present in your life,

00:42:33.434 --> 00:42:37.134
which is that I think
there are trade offs on both sides.

00:42:37.467 --> 00:42:40.567
So lots of people
will ask me questions like,

00:42:40.601 --> 00:42:42.234
do you think we should have a kid?

00:42:42.267 --> 00:42:44.934
Or do you think we should bring
a child into this world,

00:42:44.967 --> 00:42:48.734
given how much potential doom
we are headed for?

00:42:49.267 --> 00:42:55.667
And from our vantage point, you know,
in these chairs in our podcast studio,

00:42:55.701 --> 00:42:59.167
we can't make those decisions
for you, on your behalf.

00:42:59.201 --> 00:43:02.834
You'll never be able
to play out the counterfactual.

00:43:02.834 --> 00:43:08.334
But I love this idea
that comes from Cheryl Strayed,

00:43:08.367 --> 00:43:10.467
one of her advice columns
where she talks --

00:43:10.501 --> 00:43:12.834
MS: We talked about this
on the phone last night.

00:43:12.867 --> 00:43:14.734
It's my favorite quote from your book.

00:43:14.767 --> 00:43:17.834
SS: Yeah. I mean, she talks
about these ghost ships.

00:43:17.867 --> 00:43:19.667
And when we make a decision in life,

00:43:19.701 --> 00:43:21.801
it's sort of like
we're standing on this pier

00:43:21.834 --> 00:43:24.901
and we have to choose to board a ship.

00:43:25.434 --> 00:43:28.867
And that ship becomes your life,
the life that you lead.

00:43:28.901 --> 00:43:31.834
But in that exact same moment,
there is a fleet of other ships

00:43:31.834 --> 00:43:34.201
that also disembark,

00:43:34.234 --> 00:43:37.334
and they represent all the other choices
that you could have made.

00:43:37.367 --> 00:43:39.801
I could have married
this person or that person.

00:43:39.801 --> 00:43:43.567
I could have moved here or there,
taken this job or that job,

00:43:43.601 --> 00:43:46.401
decided to have kids, or not have kids.

00:43:46.734 --> 00:43:49.667
And to be a human being means

00:43:49.701 --> 00:43:53.534
being willing to wave
at those ghost ships.

00:43:54.034 --> 00:43:58.534
This open sea of the life you have
and the life you could have had,

00:43:58.567 --> 00:44:01.834
and being able to be OK
with making friends

00:44:01.867 --> 00:44:04.401
as those ships fade into the fog,

00:44:04.434 --> 00:44:06.134
into the distance.

00:44:06.134 --> 00:44:09.134
I think that is what it means
to live with uncertainty.

00:44:09.167 --> 00:44:10.701
So maybe we're wrapping up here.

00:44:10.734 --> 00:44:12.934
We can just give one piece of advice.

00:44:13.967 --> 00:44:18.334
MS: One of the questions from
Anka A was around any specific tools

00:44:18.367 --> 00:44:20.534
that we would encourage people to use

00:44:20.534 --> 00:44:23.567
to increase their capacity
to embrace uncertainty.

00:44:23.601 --> 00:44:27.067
I'll share two really quickly
and then we can end with you, Simone,

00:44:27.501 --> 00:44:31.467
The first is to do an exercise
called affect labeling.

00:44:32.301 --> 00:44:34.867
I have an entire chapter
of "The Other Side of Change"

00:44:34.867 --> 00:44:36.101
devoted to rumination

00:44:36.134 --> 00:44:38.801
because I feel like I have
a personal PhD in rumination.

00:44:38.834 --> 00:44:41.601
I am a pro, I can ruminate
until the cows come home.

00:44:41.634 --> 00:44:43.501
And so I have a whole set of strategies

00:44:43.534 --> 00:44:47.967
that articulate for how to tame
that inner chatter critic

00:44:48.001 --> 00:44:50.034
that can drive us absolutely nuts.

00:44:50.634 --> 00:44:52.967
Affect labeling is when you simply give

00:44:53.001 --> 00:44:56.067
your negative emotions specific labels.

00:44:56.101 --> 00:44:58.834
So in the aftermath of a negative change,

00:44:58.867 --> 00:45:00.834
I might feel just a cloud of negativity,

00:45:00.867 --> 00:45:05.201
but identifying that the primary emotion
I'm feeling is, for example,

00:45:05.234 --> 00:45:08.834
anger, envy, grief, sadness.

00:45:08.867 --> 00:45:11.934
That can shift our focus
away from being the emotion

00:45:11.967 --> 00:45:13.867
to simply having the emotion.

00:45:13.901 --> 00:45:17.101
And that breeds what psychologists
call psychological distance.

00:45:17.134 --> 00:45:21.034
So it helps us see our situation
from new vantage points and perspectives.

00:45:21.067 --> 00:45:25.434
The other strategy is to just
spend five minutes today

00:45:25.434 --> 00:45:27.701
engaging in a self-affirmation exercise.

00:45:27.734 --> 00:45:30.234
This is where you write down
all the traits

00:45:30.267 --> 00:45:31.701
that you value about yourself,

00:45:31.734 --> 00:45:33.434
all the identities you value,

00:45:33.467 --> 00:45:36.267
that are not threatened
by the change you're going through.

00:45:36.301 --> 00:45:38.501
So if you're having
a rough go of it at work,

00:45:38.534 --> 00:45:41.201
you might mention your membership
on the pickleball team

00:45:41.234 --> 00:45:42.401
in your local community.

00:45:42.434 --> 00:45:44.834
Or if you're struggling in a relationship,

00:45:44.867 --> 00:45:46.867
you might focus on your spiritual life.

00:45:46.901 --> 00:45:50.767
The idea behind this exercise
is that when you articulate all, you know,

00:45:50.801 --> 00:45:53.201
as Simo was talking about
with anchors, right?

00:45:53.234 --> 00:45:56.534
When you articulate all the things
that define your life,

00:45:56.567 --> 00:45:58.701
then when a change threatens one area,

00:45:58.734 --> 00:46:02.334
you don't feel entirely unmoored,

00:46:02.334 --> 00:46:04.734
you know that there are still
so many parts of you

00:46:04.767 --> 00:46:08.634
that are still very alive and robust
and steady during these moments,

00:46:08.667 --> 00:46:10.467
and it helps you zoom out

00:46:10.467 --> 00:46:12.434
from whatever loss you're experiencing

00:46:12.467 --> 00:46:16.201
and to see that you're living
a very rich and multi-dimensional life.

00:46:16.234 --> 00:46:19.334
I did this in the throes
of the pregnancy losses,

00:46:19.367 --> 00:46:22.534
where so much of my attention
was on trying to become a mom,

00:46:22.534 --> 00:46:26.534
that I lost sight of all these other
rich and beautiful identities I had.

00:46:26.901 --> 00:46:29.434
And so I found that to be
a very useful exercise.

00:46:30.067 --> 00:46:32.401
Simo, over to you for the last minute.

00:46:32.434 --> 00:46:35.467
SS: Well, maybe I'll end
with another quick story,

00:46:35.501 --> 00:46:37.667
which comes from a friend
of mine named Emily.

00:46:37.701 --> 00:46:41.001
And Emily is a therapist,
a mental health professional.

00:46:41.034 --> 00:46:42.267
But in her early 20s,

00:46:42.301 --> 00:46:46.101
her mom was given
a potentially terminal diagnosis.

00:46:46.134 --> 00:46:50.334
And so she spent weeks
by her mom's side sitting in the hospital,

00:46:50.367 --> 00:46:51.867
and she was really struggling.

00:46:51.901 --> 00:46:54.534
She was riding this
emotional roller coaster

00:46:54.567 --> 00:46:57.067
of fear and anticipatory grief.

00:46:57.067 --> 00:47:00.467
And one day, a family friend
of Emily's named Bill came to visit.

00:47:00.467 --> 00:47:02.034
And Bill is an oncology doctor.

00:47:02.067 --> 00:47:05.534
So he has lots of experience
helping people at the end of their lives.

00:47:05.567 --> 00:47:07.301
He asked Emily, "How are you doing?"

00:47:07.301 --> 00:47:09.367
And she said, "Honestly, not very well.

00:47:09.401 --> 00:47:10.567
I'm terrified.

00:47:10.601 --> 00:47:13.734
I do not know what I'll do
if my mom passes away."

00:47:14.367 --> 00:47:15.834
Bill said something very wise.

00:47:15.867 --> 00:47:21.701
He said, "Emily, the version of you
that will deal with that tragic event

00:47:21.734 --> 00:47:24.667
if or when it ever takes place,

00:47:24.701 --> 00:47:27.934
will be born into existence
in that moment.

00:47:28.667 --> 00:47:32.034
And that version of you
will have more context,

00:47:32.067 --> 00:47:33.734
more information,

00:47:33.767 --> 00:47:37.667
and be better equipped
to deal with it than you are today.

00:47:38.667 --> 00:47:44.067
You have to trust in your future self
to handle your future problems."

00:47:45.034 --> 00:47:48.034
And I love this idea of trusting
in our future selves,

00:47:48.067 --> 00:47:51.334
because so often when faced
with uncertainty or change,

00:47:51.367 --> 00:47:53.134
we rush out to meet our worry.

00:47:53.467 --> 00:47:55.667
We think of all the things
that could go wrong,

00:47:55.701 --> 00:47:59.201
our minds catastrophize,
we spiral through all of this doubt.

00:47:59.801 --> 00:48:03.501
And yet, that brings us away
from the sphere of influence

00:48:03.534 --> 00:48:05.534
that we have in the present.

00:48:05.567 --> 00:48:06.967
It brings us into the future.

00:48:06.967 --> 00:48:11.834
But how can we stay grounded
and be able to trust in our future self?

00:48:12.301 --> 00:48:15.434
And in Emily's case, she's so glad
that she followed Bill's advice

00:48:15.434 --> 00:48:18.101
because her mom ended up recovering

00:48:18.134 --> 00:48:20.601
and making [it] out of the hospital,

00:48:20.634 --> 00:48:23.134
and she didn't need to needlessly worry

00:48:23.167 --> 00:48:25.267
about something that never came to pass.

00:48:25.601 --> 00:48:29.634
So maybe take those three things with you
as you go out throughout your day.

00:48:29.667 --> 00:48:33.701
Think about how you might be able to think
about the constants in your life.

00:48:34.067 --> 00:48:37.467
Think about how you can name the actual
feelings that you're feeling

00:48:37.467 --> 00:48:39.201
and not be those feelings.

00:48:39.601 --> 00:48:42.467
And then think about how you
might be able to cultivate trust

00:48:42.467 --> 00:48:43.967
in your future self.

00:48:44.001 --> 00:48:45.601
Thank you so much for being here.

00:48:45.634 --> 00:48:47.167
Thank you TED for having us.

00:48:47.201 --> 00:48:50.067
Thank you Maya for being such
a great conversation partner.

00:48:50.101 --> 00:48:51.534
MS: Thank you so much, Simone.

00:48:51.567 --> 00:48:52.834
It was such a pleasure.

00:48:52.867 --> 00:48:54.967
